#!/usr/bin/env python3

"""tvchat-backend - chat helper for the tvchat Turbo Vision client.

Every live connection (IRC today, Delta Chat later) and all protocol work
lives here, so the C++ front end only ever sees one small, protocol-neutral
chat model.  tvchat runs it as a co-process (like tvmail and its
tvmail-backend).  Stdlib only, Python >= 3.6.

Configuration: ~/.config/tvchat/tvchat.conf ($TVCHAT_CONF overrides it for one
run; see the shipped tvchat.conf.example; the ~/.config/tvmail/tvchat.conf of
when chat was part of tvmail is read if the new one is missing).  One
[account:NAME] section per account; NO PASSWORDS in that file - they come
from the environment, ~/.mu-tickets or ~/.netrc, for the SASL login only.

Sub-commands (all reply bodies are TAB-separated lines; a TAB, newline,
carriage return or backslash inside a field is escaped as \\t \\n \\r \\\\):

  accounts                  one line per account:
                            id <TAB> protocol <TAB> state <TAB> display
                            (state: disconnected, connecting, connected, error)
  connect ACCOUNT           start connecting; replies at once, progress
                            arrives as 'state' events
  disconnect ACCOUNT
  convs [ACCOUNT]           conv <TAB> kind <TAB> unread <TAB> title
                            (conv = ACCOUNT/target; kind: server, channel,
                            direct, group; every account if none is named)
  join ACCOUNT TARGET       open a channel or a query; prints the conv id
  part CONV                 leave it
  send CONV TEXT            prints the local message id; with IRCv3
                            echo-message the server's copy arrives as an event.
                            A line may be a command: /me ACTION, /join #CHAN
                            [KEY], /part [#CHAN] [REASON], /msg NICK TEXT,
                            /nick NEW, /topic [TEXT], /quote RAW; '//x' sends
                            '/x'.  The server conversation takes commands only.
  history CONV [--before MSGID] [--limit N]
                            message lines, oldest first:
                            msgid <TAB> conv <TAB> utc-iso8601 <TAB>
                            sender-address <TAB> sender-display <TAB>
                            flags <TAB> text
                            (flags: s self, n notice/system, a /me action,
                            e encrypted, r read).  When --before reaches the
                            start of what is kept and the server has IRCv3
                            chathistory, it is asked (status 1 says so) and a
                            'history CONV before' event follows
  members CONV              address <TAB> display <TAB> role
  mark-read CONV [MSGID]
  events [--after SEQ]      queued events, oldest first: seq <TAB> type <TAB> ...
                            (state, msg, conv-new, conv-gone, members, topic,
                            unread, and history CONV latest|before: the
                            server's history was merged in - read it again)
                            --after SEQ also forgets every event up to SEQ -
                            the UI passes the last seq it applied, so nothing
                            is ever applied twice.  Empty when nothing is queued.
  ping                      print 'pong' - a liveness check
  irc-tail ACCOUNT          debugging: connect one IRC account and print its
                            events in a plain terminal; typed lines go to the
                            newest channel ('/join #chan', '/quit')
  serve                     persistent mode: one shell-quoted request (an argv)
                            per line on stdin, one framed reply per request:
                                <status> <nbytes>\\n  then <nbytes> bytes
                            A '0 5\\nready' banner is sent first, then the
                            [chat] autoconnect accounts start connecting.  'quit' (or
                            EOF) closes every connection and exits, no reply.
                            One selectors loop multiplexes stdin and every
                            protocol socket - no threads.

  irc-tail --raw ACCOUNT    the same, plus every IRC line in (<<) and out (>>)
  floyd-daemon [--conf FILE]
                            the central Floyd: no UI, runs the accounts of FILE
                            (default tvchat.conf) until SIGTERM - an IRC account
                            and a protocol = floyd account attached to it.  Only
                            here is Floyd on (elsewhere his account needs
                            local = yes), and only here can an admin (admins =
                            SERVICES-ACCOUNT, matched on the IRCv3 account tag)
                            have him ask the Claude CLI (claude = yes)

Diagnostics (before the sub-command, as tvchat passes them):
  --debug [FILE]            log to FILE (default debug.tvchat.backend.log): every request and
                            its reply status, account state changes, every IRC
                            line in and out (SASL secrets redacted), socket and
                            TLS details, and why a connection ended
  --trace [FILE]            write cProfile data to FILE on exit (default trace.tvchat.backend.log)

Exit / frame status: 0 ok, 1 user-facing error (the body says what to tell
the user), 2 internal error (bad request, bug).
"""

